Fillets: the walked intersection of a blend with the face at its end ends
where the walk first passes the end point, not where it last comes back
near it (mitcad#343).

ChFi3d_ComputeCurves intersects the blend with the face at the end by a
walk from the middle of the cap (IntWalk_PWalking) where the analytic and
GeomInt intersections fail, and cuts the walked line between its points
nearest to the cap's ends. At an OnDiff end whose contact meets the face
at the end on an edge across which its own face goes on tangentially to
that face (a rounding along an earlier rounding that is tangent to the
face at the end), the blend touches the face at the end at that point: the
two branches of the intersection cross there. The walk passed through the
point onto the other branch, wandered past the end and came back to it;
the cut then kept the wandering part (the cap 4.3e-3 off the surfaces, the
face's wire self-intersecting) where the walk, in the stripe's other
direction, had stopped at the point. The same blend walked the other way
round gave a valid result: a rounding of a band's edge along a cone and an
earlier rounding, whose import named its edges in the other order, was
invalid.

Now the line is cut where it first passes each end: walking from the
start's nearest point, the first point whose distance to the end is a
local minimum no larger than the line's step there ends it (and likewise
for the start, from the end's side). Where the line comes nearest to an
end once, that is the point taken before.

Upstream: new.

